====Verb====
{{en-verb|past2=wound|past2_qual=proscribed}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[blow]] air through a [[wind instrument]] or [[horn]] to make a [[sound]].
#* {{RQ:Burger Scott Chase|stanza=I|page=1|passage=Earl Walter '''winds''' his bugle horn; / To horſe, to horſe, halloo, halloo! / His fiery courſer ſnuffs the morn, / And thronging ſerfs their Lord purſue.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Crump Folk Going Home|page=136|author=[[w:Constance Holme|Edith Constance Holme]]|year=1913|passage=Something higher must lie at the back of that eager response to pack-music and '''winded''' horn — something born of the smell of the good earth}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1951|author=C. S. Lewis|title=Prince Caspian: The Return to Narnia
|passage="If your Majesty is ever to use the Horn," said Trufflehunter, "I think the time has now come." Caspian had of course told them of this treasure several days ago./{{...}}/"Then in the name of Aslan we will '''wind''' Queen Susan's Horn," said Caspian.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (someone) to become [[breathless]], as by a blow to the [[abdomen]], or by [[physical]] [[exertion]], running, etc.
#: {{ux|en|The boxer was '''winded''' during round two.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|British}} To cause a baby to bring up wind by patting its back after being fed.
# {{lb|en|transitive|British}} To turn a boat or ship around, so that the wind strikes it on the opposite side.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[expose]] to the wind; to [[winnow]]; to [[ventilate]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[perceive]] or [[follow]] by [[scent]].
#: {{ux|en|The hounds '''winded''' the game.}}
# {{topics|en|Rest}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[rest]] (a horse, etc.) in order to allow the breath to be recovered; to [[breathe]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To turn a windmill so that its sails face into the wind.<ref>{{cite-book|author=Rex Wailes|title=The English Windmill|year=1954|page=104|passage={{...}}if a windmill is to work as effectively as possible its sails must always face the wind squarely; to effect this some means of turning them into the wind, or winding the mill, must be used.}}</ref>

=====Usage notes=====
* The form “wound” in the past is occasionally found in reference to blowing a horn, but is often considered to be erroneous. The October 1875 issue of ''The Galaxy'' [https://books.google.com/books?id=NS8ZAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA556 disparaged this usage] as a “very ridiculous mistake” arising from a misunderstanding of the word's meaning.
* A similar [[solecism]] occurs in the use (in this sense) of the pronunciation {{IPAchar|/waɪnd/}}, sometimes heard in singing and oral reading of verse,  e.g., ''The huntsman {{IPAchar|/waɪndz/}} his horn''.
* A British canal is very often too narrow for a full-length boat to turn around.  To allow changes of direction, recesses are dug into one of the banks every few miles.  They are used by nosing the boat into the recess, and then pulling the [[stern]] around until the [[bow]] can be pulled out with the boat facing the opposite direction.  For a motorised boat, the stern is moved around by using engine power with the [[rudder]] [[hardover|hard over]]; however, for horse-drawn boats (the vast majority of boats for the first 160 years), the crew would [[pole#Verb|pole]] the stern around. It is irrelevant whether or not the wind then strikes the boat on the opposite side.  However, the poling is analogous to what would often be required to allow a sailing boat setting off from a [[mooring]] to catch the wind on the most advantageous side for a safe departure. Although there are other theories, this is probably the reason the recesses are called [[winding hole]]s.

